  "blocks": [
    {
      "id": "tjd6ptf",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "INTRODUCTION TO SOUND"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "Sound is a mechanical wave that requires a material medium to propagate and cannot travel through a vacuum.",
        "Sound waves are longitudinal waves in gases and liquids, propagating as a series of compressions and rarefactions.",
        "The speed of sound depends on the elasticity and density of the medium, being fastest in solids and slowest in gases.",
        "The human audible range is 20 Hz to 20,000 Hz; sounds below 20 Hz are infrasonic and above 20 kHz are ultrasonic.",
        "SONAR (Sound Navigation and Ranging) uses ultrasonic waves to detect underwater objects and measure distance.",
        "Loudness is determined by the amplitude of vibration, while pitch is determined by the frequency of the sound wave."
      ],
      "id": "gjw4mkj"
    },
    {
      "type": "statStrip",
      "stats": [
        {
          "label": "Audible Range",
          "value": "20 Hz - 20 kHz",
          "icon": "info"
        },
        {
          "label": "Speed in Air (0°C)",
          "value": "332 m/s",
          "icon": "check"
        },
        {
